WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.907
Lesson 1 becomes much clearer when the scattering result is treated as a distribution of outcomes that has to be explained by one atomic model.

2
00:00:05.907 --> 00:00:11.813
Main pattern means almost all go straight through.

3
00:00:11.813 --> 00:00:17.720
Most alpha particles meet very little concentrated matter on their path.

4
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:23.627
Intermediate pattern means a few are deflected.

5
00:00:23.627 --> 00:00:29.533
Some particles pass close enough to a concentrated positive region to be pushed sideways.

6
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:35.440
Rare pattern means very few rebound sharply.

7
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.347
A tiny number come extremely close to something dense enough to reverse them strongly.

8
00:00:41.347 --> 00:00:47.253
The atomic model has to explain all three scattering patterns at once.

9
00:00:47.253 --> 00:00:53.160
Keep this takeaway in view: The atomic model has to explain all three scattering patterns at once.

10
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:58.125
This diagram matters because it keeps the track statistics connected to the atomic structure claim.

11
00:00:58.125 --> 00:01:03.091
The conclusion does not come from one path.

12
00:01:03.091 --> 00:01:08.056
It comes from the whole pattern.

13
00:01:08.056 --> 00:01:13.022
Straight-through majority means mostly empty atom space.

14
00:01:13.022 --> 00:01:17.987
Most particles cross without coming close to a concentrated positive core.

15
00:01:17.987 --> 00:01:22.953
Deflected minority means localized repulsion.

16
00:01:22.953 --> 00:01:27.918
Some tracks bend because the positive charge is concentrated in one small region.

17
00:01:27.918 --> 00:01:32.884
Rare large-angle events means tiny dense nucleus.

18
00:01:32.884 --> 00:01:37.849
Only a very small target could make such strong turns so rarely.

19
00:01:37.849 --> 00:01:42.815
The nucleus model is a pattern-reading argument, not a single-event argument.

20
00:01:42.815 --> 00:01:47.780
Keep this takeaway in view: The nucleus model is a pattern-reading argument, not a single-event argument.

21
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:53.321
A target-size analogy helps because the data say two things together: strong repulsion exists, but it is encountered only rarely.

22
00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:58.862
Rare encounter means small target area.

23
00:01:58.862 --> 00:02:04.403
Only a tiny fraction of paths come close enough to the nucleus.

24
00:02:04.403 --> 00:02:09.944
Strong effect means intense concentrated repulsion.

25
00:02:09.944 --> 00:02:15.486
When a close encounter happens, the alpha particle can turn sharply.

26
00:02:15.486 --> 00:02:21.027
Lesson gain means empty space plus dense core.

27
00:02:21.027 --> 00:02:26.568
The same analogy explains both the common straight paths and the rare dramatic turns.

28
00:02:26.568 --> 00:02:32.109
The nucleus must be tiny enough to miss often and strong enough to matter when hit closely.

29
00:02:32.109 --> 00:02:37.650
Keep this takeaway in view: The nucleus must be tiny enough to miss often and strong enough to matter when hit closely.

30
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:43.512
The strongest formula scene in Lesson 1 is really a reasoning bridge.

31
00:02:43.512 --> 00:02:49.375
Larger deflections mean a closer pass to concentrated positive charge, and that only makes sense if the positive charge is confined to a tiny region.

32
00:02:49.375 --> 00:02:55.237
Geometry idea means closer pass gives stronger turn.

33
00:02:55.237 --> 00:03:01.099
Large-angle deflection is evidence of very strong nearby repulsion.

34
00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:06.962
Statistical idea means rare events imply small target size.

35
00:03:06.962 --> 00:03:12.824
The nucleus must occupy only a tiny fraction of the atom.

36
00:03:12.824 --> 00:03:18.686
Model test means plum pudding fails.

37
00:03:18.686 --> 00:03:24.548
A spread-out positive background would not produce the rare dramatic reversals.

38
00:03:24.548 --> 00:03:30.411
Keep these relations visible: small deflection means a more distant pass; large deflection means a very close approach; and rare close approaches imply a very small nucleus.

39
00:03:30.411 --> 00:03:36.273
Use the size and rarity of the deflections together.

40
00:03:36.273 --> 00:03:42.135
Big deflections without frequent occurrence require concentrated positive charge in a tiny nucleus.

41
00:03:42.135 --> 00:03:47.998
Use rarity and deflection size together when inferring the nucleus.

42
00:03:47.998 --> 00:03:53.860
Keep this takeaway in view: Use rarity and deflection size together when inferring the nucleus.

43
00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:59.611
A dependable Lesson 1 route starts with what most particles do, then adds what some particles do, then ends with the rare extreme events that force the nucleus conclusion.

44
00:03:59.611 --> 00:04:05.362
Step 1 means most go straight.

45
00:04:05.362 --> 00:04:11.113
This is the evidence for mostly empty atom space.

46
00:04:11.113 --> 00:04:16.864
Step 2 means some are deflected.

47
00:04:16.864 --> 00:04:22.616
This shows that positive charge is concentrated somewhere.

48
00:04:22.616 --> 00:04:28.367
Step 3 means a few rebound strongly.

49
00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:34.118
This forces the idea of a tiny dense massive nucleus.

50
00:04:34.118 --> 00:04:39.869
The strongest nuclear conclusion comes from the rarest tracks, but only after the whole pattern is read.

51
00:04:39.869 --> 00:04:45.620
Keep this takeaway in view: The strongest nuclear conclusion comes from the rarest tracks, but only after the whole pattern is read.

52
00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:51.263
The first deep idea is that almost all alpha particles passing through the foil means there is not a large solid positive mass spread through the atom.

53
00:04:51.263 --> 00:04:56.907
Observation means most tracks are hardly changed.

54
00:04:56.907 --> 00:05:02.550
The beam largely passes through without strong interaction.

55
00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:08.193
Inference means most of the atom is empty space.

56
00:05:08.193 --> 00:05:13.837
There is little concentrated charge or mass across most of the atomic volume.

57
00:05:13.837 --> 00:05:19.480
Lesson gain means start with the majority.

58
00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:25.123
The common outcome sets the baseline before dramatic events are added.

59
00:05:25.123 --> 00:05:30.767
The atom cannot be a uniformly filled positive object if most particles pass through easily.

60
00:05:30.767 --> 00:05:36.410
Keep this takeaway in view: The atom cannot be a uniformly filled positive object if most particles pass through easily.

61
00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:42.269
The second deep idea is that noticeable deflection needs a strong localized repulsive force, which points to concentrated positive charge.

62
00:05:42.269 --> 00:05:48.128
Observation means some tracks bend.

63
00:05:48.128 --> 00:05:53.987
Not every particle goes straight, so there must be a repelling positive region.

64
00:05:53.987 --> 00:05:59.846
Inference means positive charge is localized.

65
00:05:59.846 --> 00:06:05.704
The repulsion is stronger near one compact region rather than weakly spread everywhere.

66
00:06:05.704 --> 00:06:11.563
Misconception shield means not just positive charge somewhere.

67
00:06:11.563 --> 00:06:17.422
The pattern says concentrated positive charge, not merely the existence of charge.

68
00:06:17.422 --> 00:06:23.281
Deflection is evidence for concentrated positive charge, not for diffuse charge.

69
00:06:23.281 --> 00:06:29.140
Keep this takeaway in view: Deflection is evidence for concentrated positive charge, not for diffuse charge.

70
00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:34.292
The third deep idea is that the strongest events are very rare.

71
00:06:34.292 --> 00:06:39.444
That means the repelling region is extremely small, but when a particle comes close it is strong enough to turn it around.

72
00:06:39.444 --> 00:06:44.596
Rarity means tiny target.

73
00:06:44.596 --> 00:06:49.748
Only a small fraction of particles come close enough to the nucleus.

74
00:06:49.748 --> 00:06:54.900
Strength means dense and massive core.

75
00:06:54.900 --> 00:07:00.052
The nucleus must be concentrated enough to cause a huge deflection.

76
00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:05.204
Model verdict means plum pudding fails.

77
00:07:05.204 --> 00:07:10.356
A diffuse model could not produce these rare dramatic rebounds.

78
00:07:10.356 --> 00:07:15.508
Rare large-angle events are the sharpest evidence for a tiny dense nucleus.

79
00:07:15.508 --> 00:07:20.660
Keep this takeaway in view: Rare large-angle events are the sharpest evidence for a tiny dense nucleus.

80
00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:26.464
Use this checkpoint to test whether the learner can connect rarity and strength in one inference rather than mentioning only one of them.

81
00:07:26.464 --> 00:07:32.269
Question means rare but dramatic.

82
00:07:32.269 --> 00:07:38.073
Why do the biggest deflections tell us more than the many straight tracks on their own?

83
00:07:38.073 --> 00:07:43.878
Route means tiny target plus strong force.

84
00:07:43.878 --> 00:07:49.682
The events are rare because the nucleus is tiny, but dramatic because it is dense and strongly charged.

85
00:07:49.682 --> 00:07:55.487
Answer check means they require a concentrated nucleus.

86
00:07:55.487 --> 00:08:01.291
A spread-out model could not make such rare strong turns.

87
00:08:01.291 --> 00:08:07.096
Rare strong deflections are the evidence that forces the nucleus model.

88
00:08:07.096 --> 00:08:12.900
Keep this takeaway in view: Rare strong deflections are the evidence that forces the nucleus model.

89
00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:17.946
Carry one route into the worked example.

90
00:08:17.946 --> 00:08:22.992
Start with the straight-through majority, add the smaller deflections, then explain the rare large-angle rebounds.

91
00:08:22.992 --> 00:08:28.038
Only after all three are read should you state the atomic model.

92
00:08:28.038 --> 00:08:33.085
Step 1 means majority.

93
00:08:33.085 --> 00:08:38.131
Read what most particles do.

94
00:08:38.131 --> 00:08:43.177
Step 2 means minority.

95
00:08:43.177 --> 00:08:48.223
Read what some deflected particles show.

96
00:08:48.223 --> 00:08:53.269
Step 3 means rare extreme.

97
00:08:53.269 --> 00:08:58.315
Use the rare rebounds to force the dense-nucleus conclusion.

98
00:08:58.315 --> 00:09:03.362
Keep these relations visible: most tracks straight through -> mostly empty atom space; some tracks deflected -> concentrated positive charge; and rare large rebounds -> tiny dense nucleus.

99
00:09:03.362 --> 00:09:08.408
Do not jump straight to 'nucleus exists.' Use the whole distribution as the evidence chain.

100
00:09:08.408 --> 00:09:13.454
Rutherford answers are strongest when the evidence chain is built in order.

101
00:09:13.454 --> 00:09:18.500
Keep this takeaway in view: Rutherford answers are strongest when the evidence chain is built in order.
